WEBVTT

00:00.345 --> 00:03.153
- [Voiceover] So we've got
this infinite series here,

00:03.153 --> 00:04.986
and let's see, it looks
like a geometric series.

00:04.986 --> 00:07.323
We're gonna go from this
term to the second term,

00:07.323 --> 00:10.406
we are multiplying by negative three,

00:11.328 --> 00:13.453
and then to go to the next
term we're gonna multiply

00:13.453 --> 00:15.781
by negative three again.

00:15.781 --> 00:18.562
So it looks like we have a
common ratio of negative three.

00:18.562 --> 00:20.909
So we could actually rewrite this series

00:20.909 --> 00:23.492
as being equal to negative 0.5,

00:26.168 --> 00:30.070
I could say times negative
three to the zero power,

00:30.070 --> 00:32.820
negative three to the zero power,

00:34.213 --> 00:37.494
plus negative zero, or maybe I can just

00:37.494 --> 00:41.661
keep writing this way, minus
0.5 times negative three

00:43.986 --> 00:48.256
to the first power, times
negative three to the first power,

00:48.256 --> 00:49.089
minus 0.5,

00:50.732 --> 00:51.565
minus 0.5

00:52.821 --> 00:56.236
times negative three to the second power,

00:56.236 --> 00:58.997
negative three to the second power.

00:58.997 --> 01:01.302
And we're just gonna keep going like that.

01:01.302 --> 01:03.087
And we could just say we're
just gonna keep having

01:03.087 --> 01:05.385
minus 0.5 times negative three to each

01:05.385 --> 01:08.229
or to a higher and
higher and higher powers.

01:08.229 --> 01:10.653
Or we could write this in sigma notation.

01:10.653 --> 01:12.799
This is equal to the same thing,

01:12.799 --> 01:17.748
as the sum from, let's say
n equals zero to infinity,

01:17.748 --> 01:19.754
we're just gonna keep
going on and on forever.

01:19.754 --> 01:22.603
And it's gonna be this
first, it's gonna be,

01:22.603 --> 01:24.337
you can kind of think the
thing we're multiplying

01:24.337 --> 01:25.768
by negative three to some power,

01:25.768 --> 01:28.268
so it's gonna be negative 0.5,

01:30.200 --> 01:31.915
actually, I'm just gonna
do that yellow color.

01:31.915 --> 01:33.248
So it's gonna be

01:34.610 --> 01:37.443
negative 0.5 times negative three,

01:39.604 --> 01:43.604
negative, I'm gonna do
that blue color, so times

01:44.814 --> 01:48.015
negative three to the nth power.

01:48.015 --> 01:50.085
Here this is when n is
zero, here's n is one,

01:50.085 --> 01:52.367
here is n is equal to two.

01:52.367 --> 01:54.531
So we've been able to rewrite
this in different ways,

01:54.531 --> 01:57.301
but let's actually see
if we can evaluate this.

01:57.301 --> 02:00.936
And so we have a common
ratio of negative three.

02:00.936 --> 02:03.277
So our r here is negative three.

02:03.277 --> 02:05.435
And the first thing that
you should think about is

02:05.435 --> 02:08.055
well, in order for this to converge,

02:08.055 --> 02:12.012
our common ratio, the
magnitude of the common ratio,

02:12.012 --> 02:14.352
or the absolute value of the common ratio

02:14.352 --> 02:17.852
needs to be less than one for convergence.

02:21.359 --> 02:24.289
And what is the absolute
value of negative three?

02:24.289 --> 02:26.990
Well, the absolute value of
negative three is equal to three

02:26.990 --> 02:29.876
which is definitely not less than one.

02:29.876 --> 02:32.354
So this thing will not converge.

02:32.354 --> 02:34.771
This thing will not converge.

02:36.252 --> 02:38.304
And even if you look at
this, it makes sense,

02:38.304 --> 02:40.548
'cause the magnitudes
of each of these terms

02:40.548 --> 02:42.314
are getting larger and larger and larger.

02:42.314 --> 02:44.305
We're flipping between
adding and subtracting.

02:44.305 --> 02:46.521
But we're adding and
subtracting larger and larger

02:46.521 --> 02:48.636
and larger and larger and larger values.

02:48.636 --> 02:51.821
Intuitively, when things
converge you're kind of,

02:51.821 --> 02:55.960
each successive term tends
to get diminishingly small

02:55.960 --> 02:57.481
or maybe it cancels out in some type

02:57.481 --> 02:58.987
of an interesting way.

02:58.987 --> 03:03.154
But because the absolute
value of the common ratio is

03:04.738 --> 03:07.815
greater than or equal to
one in this situation,

03:07.815 --> 03:11.232
this is not going to converge to a value.
